Your homepage for “Rocks” should be a single, clear story: who you are, the main IT services you provide (web dev, app dev, digital marketing, SEO/GEO, general IT), proof of past work, and one or two strong CTAs, arranged in sections from top (hero) to bottom (contact). [muffingroup](https://muffingroup.com/blog/it-company-websites/)

Below is a professional, ready‑to‑use structure with example content and placement instructions.

***

## Page skeleton: what goes where

Successful IT and digital‑marketing sites use a consistent page anatomy: header/logo, hero (above the fold), service overview, detailed service blocks, case studies, testimonials, resources, and contact/footer. [wix](https://www.wix.com/blog/parts-of-a-website)

Your homepage should link out to deeper service pages if needed, but the main screen must clearly show your key offerings and why clients should trust you, supported by visible CTAs like “Start a project” or “Request a proposal.” [webflow](https://webflow.com/blog/marketing-agency-websites)

***

## 1. Header and navigation

Place this at the very top, fixed or sticky.

**What to include**

- Left: Rocks logo and wordmark.
- Center/right: simple navigation, for example:
  - Home
  - Services (dropdown: Web Development, App Development, Digital Marketing, SEO & GEO Optimization, IT Services)
  - Case Studies
  - About
  - Contact
- Rightmost: primary button “Start a project”.

**Example micro‑copy**

- Button: “Start a project”
- Menu item: “Services”
  
A clean header with minimal menu items helps visitors understand your expertise within seconds and reach key pages in 1–2 clicks. [webflow](https://webflow.com/blog/marketing-agency-websites)

***

## 2. Hero section (above the fold)

This is the first screen users see; it should explain Rocks in one glance and push a main CTA.

**Placement**

- Immediately under the header.
- Full‑width section with a strong headline, subheadline, CTA buttons, and a supporting visual (UI mockups, code, analytics graphs).

**Example content**

- Heading:  
  “Rocks builds and grows your digital presence.”
- Subheading:  
  “Full‑stack web development, app development, digital marketing, SEO and IT services tailored for modern businesses.”
- Primary CTA button:  
  “Schedule a free discovery call”
- Secondary CTA:  
  “View our services”
- Trust line under CTAs:  
  “Fast, reliable delivery for startups, SMEs and growing brands.”

This section should clearly state what you do (not just “we are your digital partner”) and provide one main action, which is a proven pattern on high‑performing IT and agency homepages. [muffingroup](https://muffingroup.com/blog/it-company-websites/)

***

## 3. Services overview strip

Right below the hero, add a quick “all services” snapshot so people see your breadth.

**Placement**

- Thin band section with icons/chips.

**Example content**

- Title: “End‑to‑end IT and marketing services”
- Short list of service chips/boxes:
  - “Web development”
  - “Mobile & web app development”
  - “Digital marketing & ads”
  - “SEO & GEO optimization”
  - “Managed IT services & support”

This overview acts as a quick map; deeper details live in the next sections or on dedicated service pages linked from here. [huemor](https://huemor.rocks/blog/service-page-design-examples/)

***

## 4. Web development & app development section

Make this your first deep section, because it’s core to your brand.

**Placement**

- Full‑width section with a two‑column layout:
  - Left: text.
  - Right: project screenshots or a small “feature box” grid.

**Example content**

- Section title: “Custom websites and web apps that perform.”
- Short paragraph:  
  “Rocks designs and develops fast, responsive websites and web applications focused on clean UI, stable performance and scalable architecture. We combine modern front‑end frameworks with robust back‑end systems so your digital product is reliable from day one.”
- Bullet/box highlights:
  - “Landing pages built to convert”
  - “Business websites with responsive design”
  - “Custom dashboards and SaaS web apps”
  - “Performance optimization and QA testing”

IT company and SaaS templates typically highlight their top 3–5 services like this, with concise benefit‑focused copy rather than long technical lists. [webflow](https://webflow.com/templates/category/professional-services-websites)

***

## 5. Digital marketing & ads section (“Ad boxes”)

Here you show how you drive traffic and conversions, and you can use card/box layouts for different ad/marketing services.

**Placement**

- Next section after development; use a card grid (3–4 boxes) for clarity.

**Example content**

- Section title: “Digital marketing that drives real results.”
- Intro paragraph:  
  “From search to social, Rocks plans and executes data‑driven campaigns that bring qualified visitors to your website or app. Our team aligns creatives, landing pages and tracking to turn clicks into customers.”
- Service boxes (each a card):
  1. **“Google & search ads”**  
     “End‑to‑end campaign setup, keyword strategy, A/B‑tested landing pages and ongoing optimization.”
  2. **“Social media campaigns”**  
     “Paid and organic strategies on platforms like Meta, LinkedIn and YouTube with clear reporting.”
  3. **“Content & email marketing”**  
     “Landing page copy, blog content and email flows designed to nurture leads and retain users.”
  4. **“Analytics & conversion optimization”**  
     “Event tracking, funnel analysis and UX improvements based on real data.”

Agency sites that convert well clearly list services and show that they connect creative work with measurable outcomes. [colorlib](https://colorlib.com/wp/digital-agency-website-examples/)

***

## 6. SEO & GEO optimization section

Since you specifically offer SEO and GEO optimization for web apps, give this its own section.

**Placement**

- Immediately after digital marketing; two‑column layout or highlight strip.

**Example content**

- Section title: “SEO and GEO optimization for web apps.”
- Short paragraph:  
  “Rocks improves your visibility in search and local results with technical SEO, on‑page optimization and GEO‑targeted strategies. We make sure your site and app are easy for both users and search engines to understand.”
- Sub‑points:
  - “Technical SEO: clean structure, performance, sitemaps and schema.”
  - “On‑page SEO: keyword‑aligned content, headings and metadata.”
  - “Local & GEO optimization: location‑targeted pages, map listings and regional keyword strategies.”
  - “Continuous monitoring: keyword tracking, ranking reports and recommendations.”

Dedicated SEO examples emphasize clear buckets of work (technical, on‑page, local) rather than vague “we do SEO,” which helps clients understand deliverables and value. [webfx](https://www.webfx.com/blog/seo/seo-examples/)

***

## 7. IT services & support section

You mentioned “all types of IT services”; gather them in a structured list so it doesn’t feel random.

**Placement**

- After SEO & GEO, as a supporting section.

**Example content**

- Section title: “Complete IT services under one roof.”
- Intro paragraph:  
  “Beyond development and marketing, Rocks provides ongoing IT support to keep your systems secure, available and up to date.”
- Service list/boxes:
  - “Infrastructure setup and maintenance”
  - “Cloud and hosting management”
  - “Security audits and monitoring”
  - “Backup, recovery and uptime checks”
  - “Helpdesk and remote support”

Best‑practice IT sites group these services under an umbrella and explain their impact on reliability and trust, which reassures non‑technical business owners. [themefisher](https://themefisher.com/it-website-template)

***

## 8. Past work / case studies (“Past projects” section)

This is where you prove your quality. Use cards with small metrics; these also serve as strong “ad‑like” visuals for your capabilities.

**Placement**

- Mid‑page, after services; a grid of 3–6 case‑study cards.

**Example content**

- Section title: “Work that speaks for itself.”
- Short intro:  
  “Explore a few recent projects where Rocks helped clients launch, scale and optimize their digital products.”
- Each case‑study box:
  - Project name + industry (e.g., “SaaS analytics dashboard – B2B”, “Local retailer website – E‑commerce”).
  - 1–2 sentences describing the challenge and solution.
  - 1–3 metrics or outcomes (e.g., “Page load time reduced by 45%”, “Lead volume up 2.3×”, “Organic traffic +60% in 6 months”).
  - CTA: “View details” or “Request a similar solution.”

Strong agency and IT sites make case studies very easy to find and use them as primary trust signals instead of just claims. [sitebuilderreport](https://www.sitebuilderreport.com/inspiration/agency-websites)

***

## 9. Testimonials and trust signals

Add quotes and logos to show social proof.

**Placement**

- Below case studies; a slider or small grid.

**Example content**

- Section title: “Clients on Rocks.”
- 2–4 testimonial cards, e.g.:  
  “Rocks helped us redesign our web app and launch a targeted search campaign. We saw faster load times and a clear jump in qualified leads.”  
  – “Founder, SaaS startup”
- Trust badges:
  - Placeholder area for “Featured on design galleries” or “Certified partners” you can fill later.
  - Space for client logos or brand names.

Marketing and IT agency sites with strong testimonials and visible logos often convert better because they reduce perceived risk. [muffingroup](https://muffingroup.com/blog/it-company-websites/)

***

## 10. Resources / blog (optional but good for SEO)

If you plan to write, add a small section.

**Placement**

- Near the bottom, before contact.

**Example content**

- Section title: “Insights from the Rocks team.”
- Brief line:  
  “Articles on web performance, UX, SEO, digital campaigns and IT reliability.”
- 3 article boxes:
  - “Checklist for launching a high‑performance web app”
  - “How local SEO and GEO optimization help service businesses grow”
  - “Design to data: connecting landing pages to real analytics”

Content and thought leadership on or near the homepage help position you as experts and support SEO. [surferseo](https://surferseo.com/blog/seo-content-writing-examples/)

***

## 11. Strong final CTA and contact form

End the page with a clear way to start.

**Placement**

- Second‑to‑last section; large CTA + embedded form.

**Example content**

- Section title: “Start your next project with Rocks.”
- Short paragraph:  
  “Tell us about your website, app or campaign. We’ll reply with a clear plan, timeline and estimate.”
- Form fields:
  - Name
  - Email
  - Company / brand
  - Project type (dropdown: Web, App, Digital marketing, SEO/GEO, IT services)
  - Short project description
- Button: “Send my project brief”
- Note: “We reply within 1–2 business days.”

High‑converting service pages embed simple forms with few fields, directly related to the page content, rather than hiding them on a separate page. [wix](https://www.wix.com/blog/parts-of-a-website)

***

## 12. Footer

Keep it clean but informative.

**Placement**

- Very bottom of the page, global across all pages.

**What to include**

- Rocks logo/wordmark.
- Short tagline: “Rocks – Web development, app development, digital marketing, SEO and IT services.”
- Links: Home, Services, Case Studies, Blog, Contact.
- Contact details: email, optional phone, location (Tamil Nadu, India).
- Copyright and basic legal links.

Footers act as secondary navigation and reassurance, helping users who scroll all the way down to still find key actions easily. [wix](https://www.wix.com/blog/parts-of-a-website)

